Mimic macOS Architecture: Adapt the macOS approach (likely WKWebView running Twilio JS) to Windows using C++ and WebView2. Keep the plugin structure and communication flow similar.
Plugin Setup:
Create a windows directory in the package.
Use Flutter’s Windows plugin template with CMakeLists.txt, .cpp, and .h files.
Register the plugin via flutter::PluginRegistrarWindows.
WebView2 Integration:
Initialize WebView2 with CreateCoreWebView2EnvironmentWithOptions and CreateCoreWebView2Controller.
Attach it to the Flutter window (registrar->GetView()->GetNativeWindow()).
Handle async creation with callback classes (e.g., EnvironmentCreatedHandler, ControllerCompletedHandler).
Twilio JS Execution:
Load an HTML file/string with <script src="https://sdk.twilio.com/js/voice/v1.10/twilio.min.js"></script>.
Embed HTML as a string or load from windows/assets/index.html (copy via CMake).
Use webview->NavigateToString for string content or webview->Navigate for file paths.
Dart-C++ Communication:
Set up a MethodChannel (e.g., "twilio_voice") in the plugin constructor.
Handle Dart calls in HandleMethodCall, executing JS via webview->ExecuteScript.
JS-C++ Events:
In JS, use window.chrome.webview.postMessage({ type: "event", data: ... }) for events (e.g., Twilio.Device.ready).
Catch these in C++ with webview->add_WebMessageReceived, parse the JSON, and relay to Dart via method_channel->InvokeMethod.
Key Methods (match macOS):
initialize(token): Run Twilio.Device.setup(token) in JS.
makeCall(token, to): Execute JS makeCall function.
Return values with ExecuteScript callbacks when needed.
Error Handling:
Check WebView2 init results (e.g., SUCCEEDED(result)).
Return errors to Dart via result->Error.
Lifecycle:
Create WebView2 in an initialize method or constructor (async).
Clean up in destructor (controller->Close(), release COM pointers).
Strings: Use std::wstring for WebView2 APIs (convert std::string from Dart args).
Extras:
